Prefer the kernel's own SoC name over the device tree compatible

On ARM, fastfetch fills the CPU name from /sys/firmware/devicetree/base/
compatible before it ever looks at /proc/cpuinfo, and the rest of the
detection is skipped once the name is set.  On this tablet that produces
"sm8550": correct as a driver binding string, useless as a name.

The kernel here reports "Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Gen 2" in the "model name"
line of /proc/cpuinfo, which is the conventional place every other tool
looks.  Consult it first and keep the device tree as the fallback it was
meant to be.

--- a/src/detection/cpu/cpu_linux.c	2026-08-07 04:15:10.962828880 +0200
+++ b/src/detection/cpu/cpu_linux.c	2026-08-07 04:15:10.962828880 +0200
@@ -838,6 +838,21 @@
         return;
     }
 
+    // A kernel that states the SoC's name outright beats a device tree
+    // compatible.  "qcom,sm8550" is the string drivers match on; "Qualcomm
+    // Snapdragon 8 Gen 2" is what the chip is called and what a person asked
+    // to see.  x86 has always had this line and every tool reads it, so when
+    // an arm64 kernel fills it in too it should win here as well.
+    {
+        FF_STRBUF_AUTO_DESTROY cpuinfo = ffStrbufCreateA(PROC_FILE_BUFFSIZ);
+        if (ffReadFileBuffer(FF_CPUINFO_PATH, &cpuinfo) &&
+            ffParsePropLines(cpuinfo.chars, "model name :", &cpu->name) &&
+            cpu->name.length > 0)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+
     // [x-vendor,x-model\0]*N
     char content[512];
     ssize_t length = ffReadFileData("/sys/firmware/devicetree/base/compatible", ARRAY_SIZE(content), content);
